    desc: "Bwami, the Lega association that orders social and moral life, teaches through objects: candidates moving up its ranks are shown miniature masks (lukwakongo), small ivory and bone figures, pendants and spoons, each tied to proverbs and only comprehensible at the appropriate grade. Ivory darkens to honey-gold under constant palm-oil handling, and objects are worn, displayed in heaps, or buried with their owners, so patina is biography. Because meanings shift by context, Lega sculpture reads as philosophy made portable — aphorism, not monument. The elegance of its miniatures made Lega ivories a connoisseur favorite, densely represented in European and American museums.",
