    sum: "Serene standing patriarchs — bearded, hands to chin or belly — honored as founding chiefs in Hemba lineage houses.",
    desc: "Hemba carvers made singiti: larger-than-life standing figures of lineage founders, marked by calm frontal faces, beards, elaborate cruciform coiffures and hands held beneath the chin or resting on the abdomen in a gesture of composed authority. Kept in the chief's house or dedicated shelters, the figures received offerings and libations and anchored succession claims — sculpture as genealogy. Their classical balance, descended from Luba court sculpture but pared to pure silhouette, made them late-19th-century trade favorites and 20th-century connoisseur icons, with the Warua Master's head often compared to Renaissance portraiture. Collections from Kinshasa to Zurich treat them as the great classicists of Congolese wood.",
