    sum: "Cylindrical carved figures whose round, kaolin-caked moon faces hold a magical charge — Teke butti of the Stanley Pool region.",
    desc: "Teke sculptors cut short, cylindrical wooden figures — some with arms akimbo, some abstracted to columns — whose flat round faces are thickly caked with kaolin and magical matter by ritual specialists, giving them their famous moon-face look; feathers and blades may sprout from the head. Called butti (singular) or biti, they embodied protecting charisma purchased by families, the white crust renewed as power accumulated. The rigid geometry — cylinder body, disc face, stub limbs — anticipated modernist abstraction so neatly that butti became 20th-century collecting icons. Examples anchor Teke holdings in Paris, Brussels and New York despite the ritual crust often being cleaned in transit.",
