    sum: "Elongated, stick-limbed figures with stretched ears and zigzag shoulders — Mumuye iagalagana guarding homes and diviners' huts.",
    desc: "Mumuye sculptors in Nigeria's eastern Benue valley created iagalagana: slender figures of extreme elongation, with compressed faces between enormous stretched ear lobes, angular zigzag shoulders and arms, and stacked scarification rings. Ranging from palm-sized to human height, they served households and diviners as guardians, weather-forecasters and healing instruments, kept in domestic shrines and carried out for judgment sessions. Unrecognized by the art market until 1968, when Parisian dealers unveiled them, Mumuye figures instantly became modernist sensations for their line-and-volume economy — sculpture that behaves like drawing. They remain one of the most distinctive Nigerian styles in international collections.",
