    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "Masquerade carving", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Angola and DR Congo (Chokwe)", g: "Angola, DR Congo, Zambia",
    p: "19th century–present", era: "living tradition",
    sum: "Serene female-ancestor masks with braided chignons, cruciform forehead marks and filed teeth — Chokwe mwana pwo danced at fertility festivals.",
    desc: "Chokwe carvers, professional and itinerant, carved the mwana pwo mask as the perfected female ancestor: an oval face with closed coffee-bean eyes, cruciform chingelyengelye scarification on the forehead, tear-line scarification on the cheeks, filed teeth showing in a subtle smile, and an elaborate braided chignon fixed with metal pins. Danced by men in knitted bodysuits at mukanda initiations and fertility festivals, pwo models feminine grace and social continuity; her male counterpart chihongo, with beard and lip plug, embodies chiefly wealth. Chokwe masks traveled early and far through Angolan trade, becoming among the most copied African mask types — a fact carvers answer with ever-finer carving. Museum examples from Luena and Kasai workshops remain canonical.",
    col: [{ h: "#4a3323", n: "carved brown" }, { h: "#f2ead9", n: "kaolin accents" }, { h: "#7a4a3a", n: "scarify red-brown" }, { h: "#2b1d12", n: "chignon black" }, { h: "#9aa0a6", n: "pin metal grey" }],
