    p: "pre-colonial to present", era: "living nomad tradition",
    sum: "Male beauty pageant of the Wodaabe — men in yellow-ochre face paint, kohl eyes and ostrich feathers roll their eyes for women judges.",
    desc: "At rainy-season gatherings (worso), Wodaabe clans hold the geerewol and yaake dances, where young men — the beauty-obsessed dancers of the Sahel — spend hours painting themselves: yellow-ochre faces, white rims around staring eyes, black lip-lines, nose-line shading to elongate the face, topped by ostrich-feather headdresses, agate beads and mirrored embroidery. Lined up in ranks, they roll eyes and flash grins in synchronized swaying while women judges from eligible lineages select lovers and husbands, reversing courtship norms. Ethnographers (Marguerite Dupire, Mette Bovin) and filmmakers made Gerewol an icon of Fulani nomad aesthetics; it survives as a living, photographed, and politically championed tradition in Niger.",
    col: [{ h: "#d9a441", n: "ochre face yellow" }, { h: "#f2ead9", n: "eye-rim white" }, { h: "#1f1a14", n: "kohl black" }, { h: "#b5651d", n: "burnt ochre" }, { h: "#6e4a1f", n: "leather tan" }],
