    p: "19th century–present", era: "living tradition",
    sum: "Regionally distinct Mossi funeral masks — tall Yatenga planks, concave Karanga faces — thick with sacrificial patina.",
    desc: "Mossi mask styles vary sharply by region: Yatenga's wan-zegda are towering plank masks crowned with female figure pairs, while southern Karanga-style masks show round concave faces under antelope horns, all kept by families of mask-owners (wemuuse) and danced at funerals and sacrifices to the earth spirits. Repetitive drum-driven dances circle the compound; masks are anointed with sacrificial blood and oils until surfaces build a thick dark crust valued as accumulated prayer. Art historian Christopher Roy's decades of fieldwork documented the regional taxonomy, which distinguishes Mossi work from neighboring Bwa and Gurunsi carving. Mossi masks remain central to funerals across the Nakomse and Tengsobeedu areas of Burkina Faso.",
