    desc: "Bwa villages northeast of the Mossi maintain dwo spirit shrines expressed through masks, the most famous being the nwantantay plank mask: a metre-tall board carved with a round concentric-circle face (the sun), checkerboard and zigzag fields, and hooked finials, painted in the classic black-white-red geometry of the Voltaic region. Dancers in fresh leaf-and-fiber costumes spin the planks with rhythmic lunges at harvest festivals and initiations, powered by flute-and-drum bands; the spinning circles blur into optical play. Christopher Roy's fieldwork canonized the style, and nwantantay now stand beside Mossi and Bobo work as the signature abstraction of Burkina Faso masquerade.",
