    p: "centuries to present (repainted annually)", era: "living vernacular tradition",
    sum: "Kassena women hand-model and paint geometric murals on adobe houses — grooved reliefs, zigzags and symbols in black, white and red.",
    desc: "In Tiébélé and Kassena settlements, women are the architects of façades: they model clay walls with raised grooves, ridges, animals and pots, then paint them in geometric signs — zigzags, moons, stars, snakes, ladders — in black, white and red earth pigments whose meanings include protection, fertility and status. After every rainy season the houses are re-plastered and repainted, making maintenance itself the art form. Flat-roofed, windowless, rounded forms give the village a sculpted-landscape look unique in West Africa, celebrated since the 1980s and fiercely guarded as living heritage. Tiébélé has become a pilgrimage site for muralists and surface designers.",
