    cat: "Architectural Style", c: "Sculpted mudbrick building", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Northern Nigeria and Niger (Hausaland)", g: "Kano, Zaria, Sokoto, Maradi",
    p: "medieval to present", era: "Sahel city-state tradition",
    sum: "Pear-shaped tubali mudbricks build spherical houses and Emirs' palaces, their walls engraved with fine linear patterns.",
    desc: "Hausa builders raise some of the Sahel's most sculptural architecture: pear-shaped tubali mudbricks are laid by master masons into domed, globe-like walls that read as stitched spheres, crowned by thatch or parapets and engraved with knife-drawn geometric patterns while the plaster is wet. Entrance halls (zaure) screen compounds; Emirs' palaces like Kano's Gidan Rumfa extend the vocabulary across courtyards, council chambers and gated walls, with pinnacled parapets and deep-set coves. Guilds transmit the craft from father to son; modern Nigerian architects (Demas Nwoko among them) cite its volumetric drama. Distinct from Djenné's flat-panel massing, Hausa building is all curve, groove and shadow — earthen sculpture you can live in.",
    col: [{ h: "#b5651d", n: "earth tan" }, { h: "#8a5a2b", n: "mudbrick brown" }, { h: "#efe6d0", n: "etched lime lines" }, { h: "#6b4a2f", n: "shade brown" }, { h: "#c9a227", n: "thatch gold" }],
