    p: "1950s–present", era: "living craft",
    sum: "Cocoa pods, airplanes, fish and Bibles carved as coffins — Ga fantasy coffins that send the dead off in their life's emblem.",
    desc: "Fantasy coffins (abebuu adekai, 'proverb boxes') began when carpenter Seth Kane Kwei built an airplane coffin around 1951 for a prominent uncle who had never flown; the idea crystallized older Ga palanquin traditions into coffin form. Families commission coffins shaped by the deceased's trade or passion — cocoa pod for farmers, fish and dugout canoes for fishermen, hens, Bibles, Mercedes cars, scuttling crabs — built in plywood and enamel with startling paintwork, then buried with the body. Kane Kwei's Teshie workshop trained Paa Joe, whose pieces entered galleries and the 1989 Magiciens de la terre exhibition; documentaries and museum shows (American Folk Art Museum) carried the craft worldwide while funerals in Accra keep it working.",
    col: [{ h: "#c0392b", n: "poppy enamel red" }, { h: "#ffd60a", n: "taxi yellow" }, { h: "#1c5d99", n: "fish blue" }, { h: "#2d6a4f", n: "cocoa-leaf green" }, { h: "#f2ead9", n: "sail white" }],
