    sum: "Enamel-painted, melodrama-maximalist canvas posters selling Nollywood films in market stalls — Africa's great vernacular key-art school.",
    desc: "When 'Living in Bondage' ignited Nigeria's home-video boom in 1992, film stalls needed walls of key art faster than print could manage: workshop painters answered with hand-painted canvases of roaring melodrama — vengeful village witches, midnight pastors, lovers in tears — rendered in fast enamel house-paint with high-key color, exaggerated facial drama and clashing display lettering. Sold and rented from Idumota in Lagos to Onitsha's main market, the posters developed a shared visual grammar: montage of plot beats, horror green and gospel white, star names in fat brush-drawn caps. Collectors and galleries now prize surviving canvases as a genuine Nigerian pop-art school, kin to hand-painted African cinema and jeepney sign-painting.",
