    desc: "The kora is a harp-lute: a large calabash cut and covered with cow skin, a notched bridge holding 21 strings traditionally of antelope hide, now nylon, and a tall notched neck. Played by jali (griots) of hereditary Mande lineages, it interlaces ostinato bass (kumbengo) with improvised flourishes (birimintingo), accompanying epic recitations of Sunjata and praise songs; its four tuning scales (silaba, tomora, hardino, sauta) structure the repertory. Sidiki Diabaté Sr. recorded its first landmark album (1970); his son Toumani Diabaté made it a global concert instrument ('The Mande Variations', 2008) and Sona Jobarteh broke gender lines. From Gambia's Griot tradition to Afro-pop collaborations, kora is West Africa's signature string voice.",
