    sum: "Hundreds of thousands of West African Arabic-script manuscripts — jurists' marginalia, geometric illumination, gold-dusted headers — from Timbuktu's library families.",
    desc: "From the 1300s onward Timbuktu's Sankoré circle and private families built libraries of hundreds of thousands of manuscripts on law (Ahmad Baba's jurisprudence), astronomy, medicine and poetry, written in a local Sudanic cursive and decorated in a distinct regional illumination: interlace knots, square kufic bands, rosettes and geometric palmettes in mineral blue, red and gold, with no figurative imagery following Islamic convention. Caregiving passed down families like the Haidara and Kati libraries; in 2012 activists smuggled hundreds of thousands of pages out of occupied Timbuktu to Bamako. UNESCO inscribed the Timbuktu Manuscripts on the Memory of the World Register in 1988, and digitization continues — West Africa's greatest manuscript civilization now photographed page by page.",
